  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Nature and Mechanism
    
    An aurora-channeling discipline that allows the practitioner to perceive and navigate ice-memory — the literal impressions of past events frozen into the Berg's structure — by inducing deliberate hypothermic numbness. The cooling spreads from the sternum outward. The practitioner's aurora-glow dims to near-invisibility, breathing becomes shallow, and for a window lasting minutes to hours, the Berg's frozen layers become navigable as though permeable. Practitioners report reading the Orca Incursion's final siege in shattered crystal, the Clever Ice Age's slow starvation etched into cavern walls, the Faction Wars' betrayals preserved in pressure-memory.
    
    The technique was discovered by Hainou mewei'Frostglass, an Aurora Stack merchant and colony-founder, near the Bastion Apex during the Frozen Peace. Its power is amplified — and perhaps originally radiated — by the Dreaming Tooth, a bone relic of orca origin held by the Wake-Singers. The Tooth's resonance can trigger the phenomenon involuntarily in nearby individuals: boundary-thinning, cooling, and unbidden ice-memory impressions without the subject's preparation or consent.
    
    ## Cost
    
    Each use exacts a cumulative physical toll. Flippers lose responsiveness. The heartbeat grows fainter. The body progressively forgets how to warm itself. Beacon-Keeper Lianhinglou, a minor Aurora Stack bureaucrat of considerable and unsettling competence, lost use of his left flipper after overuse while mapping tunnels beneath Scar☽ Sanctcaetiata. Summit-Lord Yahailong has twice been carried from the Bastion Apex half-conscious, her crew uncertain she would return fully. The deeper danger is cognitive: prolonged practitioners lose the ability to distinguish their own memories from those belonging to the ice.
    
    ## Alteration
    
    In sufficiently deep meditative states, the technique permits *rewriting* ice-memory — reshaping what the frozen record claims happened. Yahailong employed this capability to alter founding-era records concealing a fire-core diversion that cost two families their lives. True erasure appears impossible. Both original impression and revision persist in layers, producing a clouded palimpsest that degrades legibility rather than replacing content. The alteration leaves an identifiable anesthetic residue in the ice that trained readers can detect. More troubling: corrupted ice-memory contaminates subsequent readers, seeping into their thermal signature until false record and genuine discovery become indistinguishable.
    
    I have encountered this residue in the Foundation Depths. It reads like numbness wearing another impression's face. One learns to recognize it. One does not learn to trust oneself afterward.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771575174146_0", anchor: "discovered by Hainou mewei'Frostglass, an Aurora Stack merchant", text: "The Foundation Depths contain anesthetic residue — the same signature described here — in strata predating penguin settlement by geological ages. Hainou formalized a practice and gave it a name. But the ice was already numb in places before any penguin touched it. I have impressions in the deep layers I cannot attribute to any known practitioner or civilization. Something was reading memory down there before us. I recorded this in my survey notes and did not include it in the main text because I do not know what it means. I still do not.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771575174146_1", anchor: "a fire-core diversion that cost two families their lives", text: "Seven penguins. The Ouheng family: three adults, one fledgling. The Meisou family: two adults, one fledgling. A seventh name appears in the administrative ice but belongs to neither household — possibly a courier, possibly a boarder. \"Two families\" is easier to carry than seven names. I understand the impulse. I am correcting it anyway.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771575174146_2", anchor: "producing a clouded palimpsest that degrades legibility rather than replacing content", text: "Every ice-reader working below the Foundation line encounters altered strata — some from Yahailong's known interventions, some from practitioners who left no attribution. The palimpsest effect means the archive I have spent my career cataloguing contains revisions I cannot reliably distinguish from originals. This does not invalidate the work. It means the work was never what I believed it was. The ice remembers everything, including the lies told to it. Reading both has always been the task. I simply did not know that until recently.", type: :commentary, display: :full
